rpersonal (nasty remarks), memory for numbers (could affect calculations). Communication? Not mentioned but nasty remarks suggests poor crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. Strengths: situational awareness, technical cognition, leadership. Weaknesses: attitude (show-off, not open), could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weaknesses: hot temper, know-it-all attitude may affect crew cooperation.\n\nWe need best overall fit. All have some concerns. Which candidate balances? Candidate B has strong reliability and stress, but interpersonal issues severe (nasty remarks). Candidate C has strong situational awareness and leadership but attitude issues. Candidate D has responsibility and problem solving but hot temper. Candidate A has decision speed and calm but poor communication and egocentric.\n\nWhich is least concerning? Maybe Candidate D: responsibility, concentration, problem solving, adequate response to unexpected events. Hot temper is concerning but maybe manageable? Know-it-all also attitude. Candidate C's not open to new ideas also problematic. B's nasty remarks likely severe for crew cooperation. A's egocentric and poor communication also severe.\n\nThus D might be best despite hot temper. Let's propose recommendation for D, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns but weighing overall.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion: none, but we can say \"Given we have not yet discussed candidates, I propose we start with Candidate D\". That's okay.\n\nProvide evidence: D concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, responds adequately to unexpected eve